  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>The African Butterfly fish, Pantodon buchholzi, becomes about 10 cm long. It is a strictly surface-dwelling species. Males can be easily recognized by the modified anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>The specimens kept by the authors originate from Nigeria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>In the wild, it lives in quite shallow water of swamps.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>Butterfly fishes are peaceful against other fish, but they tend to be a bit anxious. It is recommended to keep them only with very calm and peaceful fish. Against conspecifics Butterfly fishes can be quarrelsome, but this behaviour is usually only evanescent.</p><p>Pantodon can really fly, he is a glider. The Butterfly fish is a strictly surface-dwelling species. The ventral fins are modified to sensitive organs that help the fish to orient. Pantodon can survive in almost oxygen-free water due to a special organ that allows the fish to breathe air that is swallowed from the water surface.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>Males can be easily recognized by the modified anal fin.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>Butterfly fishes often spawn in aquaria, but the offspring is difficult to rear, because the larvae feed exclusively from the water surface – like their parents do.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/03-bony-tongues/pantodon-buchholzi/",
      "html": "<p>There is a legend that says Pantodon was not discovered by an ichthyologist, but by a collector of butterflies. This collector caught Pantodon during a flight and thought initially the fish would be a moth.</p>"
    }
  ],
